@page "/collections"

<CatHead Title="Collections">
    Ways to show a set of things. Pick by what the reader does with it: compare values
    (a <a href="/table">table</a>), open one at a time (<code>.list</code>), follow an order
    (<code>.steps</code>), or expand into detail (<code>.accordion</code>, <code>.tree</code>).
    A record of what happened, with when, is a <a href="/timeline">timeline</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.List" Title="List" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        For rows where a table would be wrong: the fields differ per row, one field dominates and
        the rest supports it, or each row is something you <em>open</em> rather than compare down a
        column. <strong>A row that navigates is an <code>&lt;a class="list-row"&gt;</code> filling
        the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> and a row that acts is a
        <code>&lt;button class="list-row"&gt;</code></strong> &mdash; never a click handler on the
        <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> &mdash; while a non-interactive row stays a
        <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> and gets no hover, pressed or focus state. The
        <a href="/drawer">action sheet</a> is a list of button rows.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.ListLines" Title="Line count" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.list-title</code> and <code>.list-sub</code> show one line with an ellipsis until
        <code>--list-lines</code> says otherwise: a number clamps after that many lines, and
        <code>none</code> wraps freely &mdash; use it where the supporting line is a sentence, which
        a translation will make longer. <strong>Set it on the list, an <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> or a
        row</strong> to shape every line inside and align the row's leading and trailing slots to
        the first line; set it on one <code>.list-title</code> or <code>.list-sub</code> to shape
        that line alone. Row padding follows <code>[data-density]</code> wherever the list sits.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.ListCards" Title="List as cards" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.list--cards</code> presents the same rows as separate cards &mdash; a gap instead of
        dividers, each row a card surface &mdash; for a work list the reader picks from, where rows
        that run together are hard to tell apart. The markup does not change, so the button reset,
        hover, focus, <code>disabled</code>, density and sticky group headers all carry over, and
        <code>aria-current</code> or <code>aria-selected</code> draws a brand border. A
        <code>.list-meta</code> as the first child of <code>.list-main</code> is a line above the
        title.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.Steps" Title="Steps" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Progress through a sequence that has an order and an end — a wizard, an approval chain.
        State is <code>data-state</code> on the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> rather than a modifier
        class, and the number comes from a CSS counter, so inserting a step is not a renumbering
        edit.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.StickyGroups" Title="Stacking group headers" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A grouped list in a scroll container where each header pins <strong>below</strong> the one
        above it instead of replacing it, so the reader keeps the whole path to where they are —
        scroll the card. <strong>The app supplies one number and nothing else:</strong>
        <code>style="--group-head-index: 2"</code>. On a surface your own app paints, set
        <code>style="--group-head-bg: var(--surface-strong)"</code> on the list;
        <code>--group-head-fg</code> and <code>--group-head-height</code> are the same escape hatch
        for the label colour and the pinned height.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.StickyGroupsRule" Title="Group headers as a caption" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>--rule</code> trades the full-width bottom border for a hairline running from the
        label to the end of the row, so the header reads as a caption over the rows rather than a
        bar across them; use it where the groups are a state the reader scans past, not a section
        they land in. A trailing count needs <code>.sticky-group-count</code>, because the rule is
        the header's own <code>::after</code> and a pseudo-element is always the last child.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.Accordion" Title="Accordion" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Built on <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;summary&gt;</code>, so the open state, the
        keyboard operation and the announcement all come from the platform, and giving every
        <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> the same <code>name</code> makes them mutually exclusive.
        <strong>If code has to open one, bind <code>open</code> one way and never write it
        back</strong> — <code>toggle</code> fires when the framework's own diff sets the attribute,
        so a handler that mirrors the state back into a field will fight it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.Tree" Title="Tree" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Nested structure the reader expands — a group hierarchy, a folder of files: nested
        <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> inside <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, for the same reason as the
        accordion. Do <strong>not</strong> declare <code>role="tree"</code>, which promises
        single-tab-stop arrow-key navigation through a flattened view that this markup does not
        implement; <code>.tree-leaf</code> is an item with nothing under it.
    </p>
</CatExample>
